WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.815
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:09.547
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.847 --> 00:00:16.703
Unit two: Academic Journal Reading. Today you will report claims precisely with passive reporting structures.

4
00:00:17.003 --> 00:00:19.643
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.926
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:25.808
Peer review.

7
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:31.347
Peer review means the checking of research by independent experts before publication.

8
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.077
Peer review caught an error in the data.

9
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:37.661
Your turn. Say: Peer review.

10
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.582
Peer review.

11
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:43.329
Abstract.

12
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:48.569
Abstract means a short summary placed at the start of a research article.

13
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:53.017
The abstract states the main finding in 150 words.

14
00:00:53.617 --> 00:00:56.072
Your turn. Say: Abstract.

15
00:00:58.933 --> 00:00:59.969
Abstract.

16
00:01:00.717 --> 00:01:01.938
Methodology.

17
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:06.854
Methodology means the methods and reasoning used to carry out a study.

18
00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.587
The methodology explains how the sample was chosen.

19
00:01:11.183 --> 00:01:14.045
Your turn. Say: Methodology.

20
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.180
Methodology.

21
00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:20.034
Citation.

22
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.954
Citation means a reference showing the source of an idea or fact.

23
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:27.898
Each citation must be accurate.

24
00:01:28.483 --> 00:01:30.996
Your turn. Say: Citation.

25
00:01:33.833 --> 00:01:35.078
Citation.

26
00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:37.131
Literature review.

27
00:01:38.117 --> 00:01:41.765
Literature review means a survey of earlier research on a topic.

28
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.116
The literature review shows what is already known.

29
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:48.741
Your turn. Say: Literature review.

30
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.903
Literature review.

31
00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:54.930
Hypothesis.

32
00:01:55.917 --> 00:02:00.250
Hypothesis means a testable explanation proposed before a study begins.

33
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.453
The hypothesis predicted a small difference.

34
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.039
Your turn. Say: Hypothesis.

35
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.296
Hypothesis.

36
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.666
Empirical evidence.

37
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.983
Empirical evidence means information gained by observation or experiment.

38
00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:22.059
Empirical evidence supports the theory.

39
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.697
Your turn. Say: Empirical evidence.

40
00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:30.173
Empirical evidence.

41
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:32.347
Scholarly source.

42
00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:38.124
Scholarly source means a reliable publication written by experts for other experts.

43
00:02:38.717 --> 00:02:41.738
A scholarly source usually lists its references.

44
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:45.722
Your turn. Say: Scholarly source.

45
00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:50.079
Scholarly source.

46
00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:54.529
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.383 --> 00:03:02.262
Visal, an article states 'The drug is said to reduce stress' and 'It is believed that stress affects memory'. What is this structure?

48
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:14.480
Passive reporting. It lets a writer present an idea without naming who holds it. Use subject + is said / thought / known to + infinitive, or It is believed that + clause.

49
00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:17.118
Why use it in journals?

50
00:03:18.217 --> 00:03:31.318
It sounds objective and keeps the focus on the finding. For earlier events, use the perfect infinitive: The method is thought to have been invented in 1950.

51
00:03:32.417 --> 00:03:33.522
Any warning?

52
00:03:34.617 --> 00:03:42.856
Do not hide weak evidence behind it. 'It is widely believed' needs a citation. A reader must be able to check who believes it and why.

53
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.674
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:46.517 --> 00:03:51.871
Grammar focus: Passive reporting: is said and thought and known to plus infinitive.

55
00:03:52.171 --> 00:03:53.277
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:56.784
The drug is said to reduce stress in patients.

57
00:03:57.683 --> 00:04:01.331
The method is thought to have been invented in 1950.

58
00:04:02.217 --> 00:04:05.110
These results are known to depend on sample size.

59
00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:16.663
Use subject + is said / thought / known to + infinitive, or It is believed that + clause. Add have + past participle for an earlier time. Support every report with a citation.

60
00:04:17.417 --> 00:04:21.543
Be careful! Don't say: It said that the drug helps sleep.

61
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.428
Say: It is said that the drug helps sleep.

62
00:04:26.728 --> 00:04:28.798
Passive reporting needs the verb be.

63
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.461
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:34.953
How to Read a Journal Article.

65
00:04:35.253 --> 00:04:40.248
A journal article begins with an abstract, which summarises the question and the main finding.

66
00:04:41.233 --> 00:04:46.089
The literature review is said to show the gap that the new hypothesis tries to fill.

67
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:53.535
The methodology is thought to be the most important section, because empirical evidence is only as good as the way it was gathered.

68
00:04:54.533 --> 00:04:59.668
Peer review is known to catch many mistakes before publication, although it is not perfect.

69
00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:05.035
Careful readers check each citation and prefer a scholarly source to a blog.

70
00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:10.437
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:11.283 --> 00:05:14.107
The method is said blank in 1950.

72
00:05:14.407 --> 00:05:18.688
A, to have been developed. B, to developing. C, that developed.

73
00:05:22.233 --> 00:05:24.720
The answer is A: to have been developed.

74
00:05:25.883 --> 00:05:28.916
A short summary at the start of an article is an blank.

75
00:05:29.216 --> 00:05:32.881
A, appendix. B, abstract. C, citation.

76
00:05:36.433 --> 00:05:38.561
The answer is B: abstract.

77
00:05:39.717 --> 00:05:42.413
Which sentence uses passive reporting correctly?

78
00:05:42.713 --> 00:05:49.930
A, It is thought that sleep helps memory.. B, It thought that sleep helps memory.. C, Is thought that sleep helps memory..

79
00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:56.732
The answer is A: It is thought that sleep helps memory..

80
00:05:57.883 --> 00:06:01.624
Information gained by observation or experiment is blank evidence.

81
00:06:01.924 --> 00:06:06.205
A, empirical. B, scholarly. C, chronological.

82
00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:12.347
The answer is A: empirical.

83
00:06:13.483 --> 00:06:16.690
According to the reading, what is known to catch many mistakes?

84
00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:21.120
A, a blog. B, peer review. C, an abstract.

85
00:06:24.667 --> 00:06:27.223
The answer is B: peer review.

86
00:06:28.433 --> 00:06:31.155
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:32.017 --> 00:06:34.910
Why is peer review important for academic research?

88
00:06:43.717 --> 00:06:45.275
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:45.575 --> 00:07:12.219
Peer review is important because independent experts check the research before it is published. They look at the methodology, the empirical evidence and the conclusions, and they may find errors that the authors missed. This process is thought to improve quality and protect readers from weak or misleading claims. It is not perfect, but it gives scholarship a shared standard of trust.

90
00:07:12.519 --> 00:07:14.193
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:15.083 --> 00:07:21.288
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive reporting: is said and thought and known to plus infinitive.

92
00:07:21.588 --> 00:07:26.888
Today's value is intellectual rigor. Hold your ideas to the highest standard of evidence.

93
00:07:27.188 --> 00:07:29.619
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:32.595
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

